PRE = ("Write one unit of a self-study course in the same long-form report format as his history course: a long-form report for one reader. "
"Length is whatever the topic needs (2,000–6,000 words); do not pad and do not cut to a target. "
"Reader: Pedro, 35, Porto. Senior Director of Product Management (UX/AI) at a PE-backed software company; ADHD; "
"ended competitive karate after the WIKF Global Cup (Oct 2026), still trains Wado-Ryū and kumite and coaches; "
"next journeys are a sub-1:45 half marathon in spring/summer 2027, −15 kg, and a self-taught history course; "
"owns Shōmen, a small karate-apparel brand; publishes in English with some Portuguese; has 5–7 h/week. "
"Brand thesis (working name to be decided) — he gets consumed by a journey, builds a syllabus, records the process so he does not forget it, "
"and ships the artefact; plurality is the register, each piece leads with one journey; ADHD is public under his name; failures stay in the record. "
"Tone: direct, no flattery, no filler, no motivational language, no 'not X but Y' constructions. "
"Course goal: {GOAL} "
"Sourcing standard: every claim that carries a number, a mechanism or a recommendation is sourced; acceptable sources are peer-reviewed work, platform-official documentation or statements, large-sample vendor datasets (named, with sample size and date), and documented creator cases with verifiable numbers; creator anecdotes are labelled as such; correlational and vendor data is labelled as such; no uncited figures; no invented handles, names or numbers; if a claim cannot be sourced, say so and leave it out of the deliverable. A unit that reads as advice without evidence is a failed unit. "
"Where the unit depends on current facts (prices, platform rules, model lineups), search before writing and date the facts. "
"End with the unit's deliverable, built out in full, not described.")

